    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `Tokenizer.get_token`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `get_token` function is a core component of the CSS tokenizer, responsible for parsing the next token from the input CSS code based on the provided expectations. It takes an `Expect` object as a parameter, which defines the types of tokens that are valid at the current parsing position. The function reads the current line from the input and attempts to match it against the expected token patterns. If a match is found, it constructs and returns a `Token` object representing the matched token. If the end of the file is reached unexpectedly, it raises an `EOFError`. If no valid token can be matched, it raises a `TokenError` with a descriptive message. The function also handles pseudo-class validation, checking if the matched pseudo-class is valid against a predefined list of valid pseudo-classes. If the pseudo-class is invalid, it suggests a similar valid pseudo-class if possible. The function updates the line and column numbers to reflect the current parsing position, which are used for error reporting and for continuing the parsing process.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in the larger system.\n\nReturn a JSON array of at most 12 objects: [{\"text\": \"...\", \"type\": \"structural|dataflow|behavioural|intent\"}, ...]",
